questions.detailed:
**Web Components are a set of browser standards; Micro Frontends are a way of organising teams and applications.** Comparing them directly is a category error, though they work well together.

## What Web Components provide

- **Custom Elements:** define tags such as `<vehicle-card>` with lifecycle callbacks.
- **Shadow DOM:** style and DOM encapsulation.
- **HTML templates and slots:** declarative composition.

The element's attributes, properties and DOM events form a framework-neutral contract that any host can use.

## What they do not provide

Web Components do not say how a remote bundle is discovered, versioned, deployed, shared or monitored. They do not define team boundaries. You still need a loading mechanism and governance.

## Using them for Micro Frontends

```js
// Domain bundle registers an element
class FinanceEstimator extends HTMLElement {
  connectedCallback() {
    const vehicleId = this.getAttribute('vehicle-id');
    this.render(vehicleId);
  }
}
customElements.define('finance-estimator', FinanceEstimator);

// Shell page uses it, passing data in via attributes
// <finance-estimator vehicle-id="v-123"></finance-estimator>
```

The shell loads the domain script and drops the tag in. Data goes in via attributes and properties; events go out via `CustomEvent`.

## Considerations

- Shadow DOM isolates CSS, which helps avoid style leaks but makes global theming need CSS custom properties.
- Server-side rendering and some framework interop (React props as strings, events) need extra care.
- Accessibility across shadow boundaries (labels, focus) needs testing.

## Summary

Use Web Components when you need a framework-agnostic integration contract; use Module Federation when everything is in one framework and deeper sharing matters.
